<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Wettin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Wettin was a German dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. Two of its members, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a>, were elected kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> in the eighteenth century. Their reigns, during which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> exercised strong influence over the Commonwealth, are associated with its decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin.jpg" alt="House of Wettin" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wereszczyński · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COA_Wettin.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The name Saxony itself travelled: carried by the electoral title of Saxe-Wittenberg to the <b>Wettin</b> lands on the upper Elbe, it now names a German state far from the original duchy, whose heartland became Lower Saxony and Westphalia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Ascanian electoral line died out with Albert III in 1422, Emperor Sigismund granted the electorate in 1423 to Frederick the Warlike, Margrave of Meissen of the <b>House of Wettin</b>, as a reward for his support against the Hussites.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A treaty of 1554 settled inheritance on the <b>Wettins</b>, and when George Ernest died without heirs in 1583, the county passed to the Ernestine and Albertine branches of Saxony, with parts to Hesse.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-henneberg">County of Henneberg</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts were vassals of greater neighbours, including the bishops of Halberstadt and the <b>Wettins</b>, and repeatedly pledged or sold lands to pay debts.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-hohnstein">County of Hohnstein</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts were among the leading Thuringian nobles alongside the <b>Wettins</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-schwarzburg">County of Schwarzburg</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Ernestine Wettins</b>, who lost the electorate in 1547, kept lands in Thuringia that were repeatedly divided.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weimar">Duchy of Saxe-Weimar</a></li></ul></section>
